{"id": 351692, "name": "Detheridge vs. Earle", "name_abbreviation": "Detheridge v. Earle", "decision_date": "1873-06-06", "docket_number": "", "first_page": "310", "last_page": "311", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "4 S.C. 310"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [{"cite": "3 S. C., 396", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [2341324], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/3/0396-01"]}], "analysis": {"cardinality": 154, "char_count": 1929, "ocr_confidence": 0.501, "pagerank": {"raw": 5.298132930532853e-08, "percentile": 0.33173439841473384}, "sha256": "88bd1b1358a5ccfe1039e214c6907745affc4bd3cf80957b251a7f22844983d9", "simhash": "1:b2b92fabe8a50a18", "word_count": 354}, "last_updated": "2024-02-27T22:44:38.979952+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": ["Moses, C. J., and Willard, A. J., concurred."], "parties": ["Detheridge vs. Earle."], "opinions": [{"text": "The opinion of the Court -was delivered by\nWright, A. J.\nThis case was heard by this Court at the April Term, 1872, and sent to the Circuit Court for a new trial.\nOn the call of the docket of that Court, the plaintiff being called and not answering, the following order in the cause was made by the presiding Judge : \u201c The plaintiff and plaintiff\u2019s counsel having been called on this the last day of the attendance of the jurors, and upon a peremptory call of the docket, on motion of Earle and Blythe, the defendant\u2019s attorneys, it is ordered that the plaintiff do pay the costs of this action, except the costs of appeal, on or by fifteenth day of October, prox., and in default thereof that he be non-suited.\u201d The order presupposes that the plaintiff was in default. This is not shown by anything in the brief.\nIt does not appear that notice of trial had ever been given by the defendant as required by the 277th Section of the Code of Procedure, page 633 of new edition of General Statutes. He could take no advantage either of the unwillingness or the inability of the plaintiff to nroceed to trial, because he was not in a position to avail himself of the benefit to which he might have been entitled, if he had shown that he ever gave notice that he would require a trial.\nThe motion is granted, and the case remanded to the Circuit Court for the County of Greenville.\nMoses, C. J., and Willard, A. J., concurred.", "type": "majority", "author": "Wright, A. J."}], "attorneys": ["Stohes, for appellant.", "Earle & Blythe, contra."], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "HEARD APRIL TERM, 1873.\nDetheridge vs. Earle.\nA defendant cannot avail himself of plaintiff's failure to proceed to trial on the call of the case and demand a non-suit unless he himself has given notice of trial as required by Section 277 of the Code of Procedure.\nBefore ORR, J., at Greenville, September Term, 1872.\nThe case on the first appeal is reported in 3 S. C., 396, and the whole case as now made is stated in the judgment of the Court.\nStohes, for appellant.\nEarle & Blythe, contra.\nJune 6, 1873."}, "file_name": "0310-01", "first_page_order": 318, "last_page_order": 319}